== 15. Of the Master

Next to parents and children, by whom the family is increased, comes a second sort of couple, which are helps to it. And they are masters and servants.

The master is a member of the family who has power and bears rule over the servant. And his duty stands chiefly in three things.

First, to make a good choice of his servants. This is done when he looks first for those who fear God and are willing to serve him. Paul makes the service and fear of God the main ground of true obedience in servants (Eph 6:5, 6, 7; Col 3:22). It was the rule of David’s choice. Ps 101:6: “He who walks in the way of integrity shall serve me.” Abraham’s chief servant of his house was a man who feared God, as appears in that he made conscience of his oath (Gen 24:2, 3), and also prayed for success in the business he was sent about. Gen 24:12: “O #smallcaps[Lord], God of my master Abraham, please grant me success today, and show kindness to my master Abraham.” Cornelius of Caesarea, the centurion of the Italian Regiment, had a soldier who attended on him daily and who feared God, and servants besides him, to whom he made known the heavenly vision he had seen (Acts 10:7).

Secondly, to assign them their labor, and not to require more of them than their strength will bear. The master is to rule over the servant #emph[in justice.] And his command is unjust when it will not stand with the course of nature, with the ability of his servant, or with the word of God. Therefore he is to require of them labor in proportion to their strength, and to give them times of respite and rest. Lev 25:46: “But as for your#super[+] brothers, the Israelites, no man may rule harshly over his brother.” 1 Pet 3:8: “Be … sympathetic, … be tenderhearted and humble.” And for the furthering of business it is fitting that the master be often present with the servants at their work. Prov 27:23: “Be sure to know the state of your flocks, and pay close attention to your herds.” The good matron “watches over the affairs of her household” (Prov 31:27).#footnote[Arist. Oeconom. 7.] A man of Libya was asked what it was that might make a horse fat. He answered, “The master’s eye.”

Thirdly, to reward the diligence and pains of his servant, and that in three ways.

First, by giving him his due of food and drink at the present time. The good matron provides “food for her household and portions for her maidservants” (Prov 31:15).

#emph[Secondly, by paying him his wages at the end of his service.] “The worker is worthy of his wages” (Luke 10:7). “Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things; I will put you in charge of many things. Enter into the joy of your master!” (Matt 25:21).

#emph[Here three caveats are to be observed. I. That the wages be in proportion to the work. II. That they be paid in due time, without delay. So the master of the vineyard,] “when evening came,” #emph[called his servants together to] “pay them their wages” (Matt 20:8). #emph[III. That the servant not be defrauded of any part of his due. For this is a crying sin.] Deut 24:15: “You are to pay his wages each day before sunset, … Otherwise he may cry out to the #smallcaps[Lord] against you, and you will be guilty of sin.” Jas 5:4: “Look, the wages you#super[+] withheld by fraud from the workmen who mowed your#super[+] fields are crying out against you#super[+]. The cries of the harvesters have reached the ears of the Lord of Hosts.”

Thirdly, if the servant is sick during his time of service, the master’s care must be to procure his recovery by all possible means. Equity must be the rule in these cases, and masters are to give their servants “what is right and fair” (Col 4:1). Now the servant perhaps falls sick through his service and while in it. And his health brings not only profit to his master, but also encouragement to himself. The good centurion, when his servant was sick, took the best course to have him restored (Matt 8:6).
